{
  "dom_id": "step-q215489",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Solve the absolute value inequality $1+|17-3h|\\le 9.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$h\\le $\n{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+|17-3h| & \\le 9 \\\\ |17-3h| & \\le 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $17-3h$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $8.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-8\\le 17-3h\\le 8\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8\\le 17-3h & \\le 8 \\\\ -25\\le -3h & \\le -9 \\\\ 25\\ge 3h & \\ge 9 \\\\ \\frac{25}{3}\\ge h & \\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the smaller number is on the left:\n\n$$\n3\\le h\\le \\frac{25}{3}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
